study, gene, or protein — and noting a negative constraint that this relational override shouldn't kick in for things like \"X patients\" or measure compounds like \"X risk,\" which gold data treats as Modifier regardless of adjacency to a relational phrase. I want the subject/topic position to still count as SpecificDisease no matter what's adjacent.\n\nI'm deciding to skip a separate \"validity\" sub-bullet since that's more of an internal consistency check than something that needs to appear in the output — the task just wants the single general principle as a markdown list, so I'll finalize the wording now to keep it clean and actionable.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Relational Argument vs. Attributive Classifier:** IF a disease name is (a) the complement of a relational or clinical-history noun, such as a prepositional phrase after a noun of history, association, exposure, or causation, or (b) the target of a causal or resultative participle, such as \"-causing\" or \"caused by,\" THEN label it **SpecificDisease**, even when it sits directly before another noun, because it is the entity being referenced. IF the disease name is a noun-adjunct premodifier that only restricts a head denoting a population, case set, study, patient group, gene, or protein, THEN label it **Modifier**.\n  - **Negative constraint:** Do not apply the relational override when the disease name only classifies a group, study, gene, or molecule head, or when it forms a measure-type compound such as \"[disease] risk,\" which the gold standard labels Modifier. Adjacency to a noun is not sufficient evidence for either label. A disease that is the topic or subject of its clause remains SpecificDisease regardless of its neighbors. If the same string receives different labels within one document, defer to the annotation guidelines rather than this rule.\n  - **Generalization:** Classify by the syntactic role of the disease string (complement of a relational noun, object of a causal participle, or noun-adjunct premodifier) and by the semantic class of the head noun (population/unit/molecule versus relational or causal argument), not by lexical identity or surface adjacency.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1947, "output_tokens": 2437, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 1965}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}